.TH CCPOLYTEST 1
.SH NAME
ccpolytest \- test keyring->ccpolysetup() and the ChaCha20-Poly1305 API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccpolytest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ccpolytest
checks
.BR ccpolysetup() ,
.BR ccpoly_setiv() ,
.BR ccpoly_encrypt() ,
and
.B ccpoly_decrypt()
.RI ( module/keyring.m ,
.IR libinterp/keyring.c ,
.IR libsec/ccpoly.c ,
.IR libsec/poly1305.c ,
ported from 9front's own
.B libsec/port/ccpoly.c
and
.B poly1305.c
\(em see
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree) \(em primitive 6 of the SSL modernization plan,
needed for TLS 1.2's
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305
record protection, the suite
.B mastodon.social
was measured to negotiate. Raw ChaCha20 itself
.RI ( libsec/chacha.c )
predates this plan and is untouched by it; only the Poly1305 MAC and
the AEAD wrapper combining the two were added.
.PP
.B Poly1305state
is a small state struct private to
.B poly1305.c
and
.BR ccpoly.c ,
not the shared
.B DigestState
every hash/HMAC in this tree already uses \(em its
.B state[5]
is too small for Poly1305's 14 words of key and accumulator, and
other code
.RI ( sha1pickle.c , " md5pickle.c" )
already depends on
.BR DigestState 's
exact size, so growing it was judged a bigger and riskier change than
this primitive calls for.
.PP
Four cases check
.B ccpoly_encrypt()
and
.B ccpoly_decrypt()
together: RFC 8439 \(sc2.8.2's own published worked example (key, IV,
AAD, plaintext, and expected ciphertext/tag, taken directly from the
standard, not just a from-scratch reimplementation of its text);
empty plaintext and AAD; a 37-byte plaintext (a partial final block,
exercising Poly1305's own padding path) with no AAD; and a 20-byte
plaintext (partial final block) with AAD. The latter three vectors
were generated from Python's
.B cryptography
library, then cross-checked against
.B pycryptodome
\(em a second, independent implementation \(em agreeing byte for byte
before being trusted, the same discipline as
.IR aesgcmtest (1).
.PP
A fifth case flips one byte of a genuine tag and confirms
.B ccpoly_decrypt()
rejects it (via
.IR tsmemcmp (3))
instead of silently returning corrupted-looking success.
.PP
A sixth case exercises
.B ccpoly_setiv()
directly \(em the entry point a real TLS record layer calls once per
record on one long-lived state \(em encrypting twice on the same
state with a
.B ccpoly_setiv()
call in between, and confirming the second ciphertext matches what a
fresh IV should produce rather than the first record's keystream
(skipping this call on a real connection is the same catastrophic
nonce-reuse failure GCM has). A seventh confirms
.B ccpoly_setiv()
rejects an IV whose length doesn't match how the state was actually
set up \(em found as a real bug during this primitive's review:
.B chacha_setiv()
reads exactly
.B state.ivwords*4
bytes fixed at
.B ccpolysetup()
time, so an 8-byte IV against a 12-byte-ivwords state read 4 bytes
past the end of a Limbo array before this check existed.
.PP
Exits with
.B PASS
if every case held, or
.B FAIL
naming which one and what was expected versus computed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ccpolytest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR aesgcmtest (1),
.IR x25519test (1),
.IR tlshellotest (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise the 64-bit (non-RFC-8439) nonce construction
.RI ( Chachastate.ivwords
== 2, the original ChaCha20-Poly1305 draft's shape) \(em all four
cases use the 96-bit nonce TLS 1.2 actually negotiates.
